    "username": "emollick",
    "nickname": "Ethan Mollick",
    "text": "A real issue with AI adoption is that organizations are built around a narrow expected range of human productivity in a role.\n\nToo little output is a problem, yes, but way too much can sometimes be as bad since approvals, staffing models &amp; coordination systems cannot absorb it.",
    "quoted_tweet": null,
    "url": "https://twitter.com/emollick/status/2082935598768091518",
    "time": "2026-07-31 05:05",
    "created_at": "Thu Jul 30 21:05:20 +0000 2026",
    "id_str": "2082935598768091518",
    "is_retweet": false
  },
  {
    "username": "emollick",
    "nickname": "Ethan Mollick",
    "text": "When workers gain productivity from AI, the rest of the bottlenecks in the system can eat up any gain. This is just a legacy consequence of human intelligence being the only kind we ever had until now, and so our current organizations are built around its limits and advantages.",
    "quoted_tweet": null,
    "url": "https://twitter.com/emollick/status/2082942606590300191",
    "time": "2026-07-31 05:33",
    "created_at": "Thu Jul 30 21:33:11 +0000 2026",
    "id_str": "2082942606590300191",
    "is_retweet": false
  },
  {
    "username": "emollick",
    "nickname": "Ethan Mollick",
    "text": "I thought sycophantic models were bad but then they all got nit-picky instead &amp; now I yearn for simple agreement rather than really smart telling me the “honest truth” about minor things I missed.\n\n(This is only a little bit a joke)",
